L000117	\author{Simon Trebst}
L000118	\affiliation{
L000119	  Institute for Theoretical Physics, University of Cologne, D-50937 Cologne,
L000120	Germany }
L000121	
L000122	\author{Christiane P. Koch}
L000123	\email{christiane.koch@fu-berlin.de}
L000124	\affiliation{
L000125	  Dahlem Center for Complex Quantum Systems and Fachbereich Physik, Freie
L000126	Universit\"{a}t Berlin, D-14195 Berlin, Germany }
L000127	
L000128	\date{\today}
L000129	
L000130	
L000131	\begin{abstract}
L000132	  Transmon qubits arise from the quantization of nonlinear resonators, systems that are prone to the buildup of  strong, possibly chaotic, fluctuations. Such instabilities will likely affect fast gate operations which involve the transient population of higher excited states outside  the computational subspace. Here we show that a statistical analysis of the instantaneous eigenphases of the time evolution operator, in particular of their curvatures, allows for identifying the subspace affected by chaotic fluctuations.
L000133	  Our analysis shows that fast entangling gates, operating at speeds close to the so-called quantum speed limit, contain transient regimes where the dynamics indeed becomes partially chaotic for just two transmons.
L000134	\end{abstract}
L000135	
L000136	
L000137	\maketitle
L000138	
L000139	
L000140	
L000141	\paragraph{Introduction}
L000142	In coupled superconducting circuits, one of the leading platforms in quantum
L000143	information science~\cite{krantz2019quantum, garcia2022quantum}, the nonlinearity of Josephson junctions serves to separate an
L000144	energetically low-lying qubit subspace from a higher-lying state space whose
L000145	presence has become a focus of attention~\cite{Berke2022,CohenPRXQ2023,Borner2023classical}.
L000146	The restriction to the computational subspace
L000147	rests on the assumption that the energy stored in the initial state remains
L000148	decentralized, and higher excited states of individual `qubits' are not
L000149	accessed. However,
L000150	residual qubit-qubit couplings can be made algebraically, but never
L000151	exponentially, small in the detuning from the resonator frequencies. Indeed, the presence of the higher-lying state space may be detrimental for stationary states relevant to storage of information, in particular so for fixed-frequency architectures~\cite{Berke2022}. While, for information storage, tunable couplers present one route to alleviate signatures of non-integrability, the issue of instabilities due to the non-linearity becomes all the more pressing for the implementation of gates.
L000152	
L000153	Indeed, the population of energy levels outside of the computational subspace is rather common when implementing fast gates~\cite{StrauchPRL03} or resetting superconducting qubits~\cite{MagnardPRL18}.  Fast
L000154	device operation is desirable in view of optimal clock speeds but also to combat
L000155	decoherence. The shortest possible time in which a given task can be carried out
L000156	is referred to as the quantum speed limit (QSL)~\cite{Deffner2017}. The QSL for
L000157	quantum gates is fundamentally determined by the size of
L000158	the effective time-dependent qubit-qubit couplings which
L000159	are needed to generate entanglement --- or, in the case of local
L000160	rotation gates, to undo undesired entanglement due to residual
L000161	couplings. Operation at the QSL is enabled by suitably tailored pulses
L000162	which bring the population of higher lying levels during the gate
L000163	operation back to the computational subspace at the end of
L000164	the gate~\cite{Goerz2017}.
L000165	The corresponding time evolution, under the perspective of (classical) nonlinear dynamics, takes, however, place in a twilight zone between integrable dynamics in the limit of weak inter-transmon coupling and possibly chaotic fluctuations at larger couplings~\cite{CohenPRXQ2023,Borner2023classical}. Quantum
L000166	mechanically, this ambiguity shows in the structure of transmon many-body
L000167	spectra which generically show statistical signatures of both integrability
L000168	(evidenced by Poisson statistics) and chaos (Wigner-Dyson
L000169	statistics)~\cite{Berke2022}. A transmon gate is then a driven protocol transiently exciting a subsystem of qubits close to the ones targeted by the gate operation. The faster a gate operation, the higher the chances that it connects to semiclassically unstable sectors of the state space~\cite{CohenPRXQ2023}. For gates transiently populating part of the higher-lying state space, instabilities may thus become relevant already in architectures where the computational subspace itself is operated in a stability island~\cite{Berke2022}. While not necessarily reflected in the gate fidelities, the presence of such instabilities will likely impact gate implementation.
L000170	It is thus imperative to clarify the role of the nonlinearity for the dynamics of coupled transmons.
L000171	
L000172	Here, we show how to analyse and detect signatures of non-integrability in a universal set of transmon qubit gates. We adapt methodology from quantum nonlinear dynamics and many-body localization theory to the unitary operators $\op{U}(t)$ implementing single-qubit and two-qubit gates. These time evolutions are realized by time-dependent external fields, designed with optimal control~\cite{Koch2022} as a map on the computational state space, but transiently acting in the larger space of high-lying states. To be specific, we consider the smallest possible structure comprising two transmon qubits coupled via an intermediate cavity subject to a microwave drive~\cite{Goerz2017}, see Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}.
L000173	We find that while traces of  nonlinear and even chaotic
L000174	dynamics are visible in the operators implementing fast gates,
L000175	they present no principal obstruction to high gate fidelities. The reason is
L000176	the finite dimensionality of the effective Hilbert space subject to control.
L000177	While classical  chaotic time evolution is unpredictable by definition,
L000178	projection onto a finite dimensional quantum Hilbert space leads to more manageable dynamics, which can be optimized towards a desired output. Crucially, however,
L000179	nonlinearity correlates with a high sensitivity of gate fidelities to just very
L000180	small modifications in the Hamiltonian parameters.
L000181	
L000182	
L000183	
L000184	\paragraph{Model and control protocols}\label{sec:model}
L000185	
L000186	\begin{figure}[tb]
L000187	    \centering
L000188	   \includegraphics[width = \columnwidth]{figures/fig1.pdf}
L000189	    \caption{
L000190	      \textbf{Spectral analysis of chaotic fluctuations in single-qubit and two-qubit  gates}
L000191	    (a--f) The statistics of the relative eigenphase differences of the time evolution are compared to Poisson (POI), resp.  Gaussian (GUE) statistics using the Kullback-Leibler (KL)
L000192	  divergence for gate durations of $T = \SI{50}{\nano\second}$
L000193	  (left) and $T = \SI{100}{\nano\second}$ (right). Deviations from Poisson
L000194	  statistics are observed in panels (a), (b) and (c) and to a lesser extent
L000195	  in panel (f).
L000196	  (g) Pictorial view of two transmon qubits
L000197	  coupled via a joint transmission line cavity. Gates are implemented by applying a fine-tuned microwave field driving the cavity~\cite{Goerz2017}, shown in (h) for the fast entangling BGATE (c).
L000198	  }
L000199	  \label{fig:setup+KL}
L000200	  \end{figure}
L000201	We analyze two universal sets of gates, with Hadamard and local phase gates
L000202	as representative single-qubit operations and the BGATE as
L000203	entangling operation. The latter yields the smallest decomposition of an arbitrary two-qubit operation into gates
L000204	of a universal set~\cite{ZhangPRL04}. For full operability of
L000205	the quantum hardware, it is important to inspect a complete universal set, as
L000206	this requires the ability to both create and destroy entanglement.
L000207	The Hamiltonian for two transmon qubits
L000208	with a joint transmission line cavity subject to a microwave
L000209	drive in a frame rotating with the drive frequency
L000210	$\omega_{\rmd}$ reads~\cite{Goerz2017}
L000211	\begin{eqnarray}
L000212	  \op{H}(t) &=& \sum_{q=1}^{2} \left[
L000213	    \delta_{q} \op{b}_{q}^{\dagger} \op{b}_{q}^{\hphantom{\dagger}} +
L000214	    \frac{\alpha_{q}}{2} \op{b}_{q}^{\dagger} \op{b}_{q}^{\dagger}
L000215	    \op{b}_{q}^{\hphantom{\dagger}} \op{b}_{q}^{\hphantom{\dagger}} + g
L000216	    \left(\op{b}_{q}^{\dagger} \op{a} + \op{b}_{q} \op{a}^{\dagger}\right)
L000217	    \right] \nonumber \\
L000218	  && + \delta_{\mathrm{c}} \op{a}^{\dagger} \op{a} + \frac{1}{2} \pulse(t)
L000219	  \op{a} + \frac{1}{2} \pulse^{*}(t) \op{a}^{\dagger}\,,
L000220	  \label{eq:H'}
L000221	\end{eqnarray}
L000222	where $\op{b}_{q}$ and $\op{a}$ are the annihilation operators for the $q$th
L000223	transmon and the cavity, respectively, $\delta_{q} = \omega_{q} - \omega_{\rmd}$
L000224	and $\delta_{\rmc} = \omega_{\rmc} - \omega_{\rmd}$ are the qubit and cavity
L000225	detunings from $\omega_{\rmd}$, $\alpha_{q}$ the qubit
L000226	anharmonicities, and $g$ is the qubit-cavity coupling strength. $\pulse(t)$
L000227	denotes the amplitude of the cavity drive with the time-dependency derived via gate optimization~\cite{Goerz2017}.
L000228	
L000229	The quantum gates we analyze operate in the quasi-dispersive
L000230	straddling qutrits (QuaDiSQ) regime where
L000231	the cavity cannot be adiabatically
L000232	eliminated and transmon levels outside the computational subspace are exploited for fast gate operation~\cite{Goerz2017} (with dim$\mathcal H=150$, see the supplemental material (SM)~\cite{SM} for details).
L000233	
L000234	The fasted possible universal set of gates uses gate durations of $T =
L000235	\SI{50}{\nano\second}$~\cite{Goerz2017}.  This limit is set by the local gates,
L000236	whereas the fastest entangling operation, $\sqrt{i\mathsf{SWAP}}$, requires only
L000237	about $\SI{10}{\nano\second}$~\cite{Goerz2017}. Fast gate operations come at
L000238	the expense of complex and spectrally broad pulse shapes $\pulse(t)$, see
L000239	Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(h) for an example. Increasing the gate durations by a
L000240	factor of two to $T = \SI{100}{\nano\second}$ significantly reduces both
L000241	temporal and spectral complexity of the pulses~\cite{Goerz2017}.
L000242	
L000243	
L000244	
L000245	\paragraph{Spectral analysis}
L000246	\label{subsec:KL}
L000247	The time evolution operators $\op{U}(t)$
L000248	corresponding to the gate operations
L000249	contain information about the entire dynamics up to time $t$, whereas
L000250	the instantaneous eigenvalues of the Hamiltonian
L000251	~\eqref{eq:H'} capture only time-local information.
L000252	We therefore analyze the statistical properties of the instantaneous
L000253	eigenvalues of $\op{U}(t)$, using the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence~\cite{Kullback1951} to quantify the deviation of their probability distribution from two limiting cases of Poisson (POI) and Gaussian (GUE) statistics for integrable, resp. chaotic, dynamics.
L000254	Figure~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(a)--(f) shows the two corresponding KL divergences $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$
L000255	(see SM~\cite{SM} for computational details):
L000256	While
L000257	$D_{\mathrm{KL}}
L000258	(P_{\mathrm{rel.}} | P_{\mathrm{POI}})$ vanishes
L000259	almost everywhere for  the slower set of gates (Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(d)-(f)), the gates at the quantum speed limit (Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(a)-(c)) show chaotic fluctuations.
L000260	
L000261	\begin{figure*}[tb!]
L000262	  \centering
L000263	  \includegraphics[scale=0.12]{figures/fig2.pdf}
L000264	  \caption{
L000265	    {\bf Detailed analysis of the fast entangling BGATE}
L000266	    (c): KL divergences as in
L000267	    Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(c) (red dotted line: end of pulse).
L000268	    (a),(b): Instantaneous eigenphases $\{\phi_{n}(t)\}$ of $\op{U}(t)$ (blue lines)
L000269	    with their population $p_{i}(t)$ indicated by size and color of the dots.
L000270	    (d)--(g): Probability distributions of the relative eigenphases
L000271	    in the time intervals highlighted in
L000272	    gray in (b), compared to ideal Poisson (blue) and Gaussian (red) distributions. (h)--(k): Dimensionless level curvatures $k_n(t)$ for the intervals analyzed in (d)--(g). }
L000273	  \label{fig:BGATE}
L000274	\end{figure*}
L000275	Figure~\ref{fig:BGATE}  examines the BGATE, one of the fast gates with chaotic fluctuations, more closely. Panel~
L000276	(a) displays the eigenphases $\phi_{n}(t)$ of $\op{U}(t)$ as blue lines, appearing as blue areas  due to their high density, and  the color code showing exemplarily the evolution of the computational basis state $\ket{00}$ (where larger and darker (smaller and brighter) dots indicate a larger (smaller)  overlap
L000277	of the time-evolved state $\ket{\Psi_{00}(t)} = \op{U}(t) \ket{00}$ with the eigenstate $\ket{\Phi_{n}(t)}$ to which the blue line corresponds).
L000278	Figure~\ref{fig:BGATE}
L000279	(b) provides a blow-up of the first
L000280	$\SI{5}{\nano\second}$ of the dynamics. This time frame is
L000281	interesting because it shows the transition from straight lines and line
L000282	crossings for all $\phi_{n}(t)$ into a regime where avoided crossings between
L000283	lines start to become more frequent.
L000284	The emerging many-body nonlinear behavior is
L000285	witnessed by the KL divergence of the eigenphases $\phi_{n}(t)$
L000286	in Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}(c).
L000287	While up to $t \approx \SI{3}{\nano\second}$, the KL divergence shows an
L000288	almost perfect match with an ideal POI statistics, evidencing integrable
L000289	behavior, it diverges from POI statistics and shows a larger match with GUE
L000290	statistics, signalling emergent chaotic behavior, between $\SI{3}{\nano\second}$ and
L000291	$\SI{4}{\nano\second}$. Figure~\ref{fig:BGATE}
L000292	(d)--(g) shows the actual
L000293	distributions at four representative times,
L000294	with an almost perfect match with ideal POI statistics before avoided crossings play a role
L000295	(d). Similarities of
L000296	$P_{\mathrm{rel.}}$ to POI statistics also dominate just before
L000297	(e) and after (g) the chaotic regime, whereas  a larger (but far from perfect)  match with GUE statistics
L000298	is observed in
L000299	(f). Interestingly, after
L000300	$\SI{4}{\nano\second}$ the dynamics becomes less chaotic again. Such emergence
L000301	and disappearance of chaos occurs several times over the entire protocol
L000302	and is also observed for the other fast gates with chaotic behavior in Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}. Remarkably, the
L000303	(re)emergence of chaotic behavior does not have any negative impact on the gate
L000304	errors --- these are identically small for both fast and slow protocols
L000305	in Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}. The optimized control field seems to navigate the
L000306	computational basis states safely through the chaotic regions.
L000307	
L000308	
L000309	
L000310	\paragraph{Curvature distributions}
L000311	\label{subsec:Curvature}
L000312	
L000313	Beyond the statistics of eigenphases, the structure of avoided crossings can be
L000314	another indicator of nonlinear dynamics.
L000315	Correlations lead to effective phase repulsion and to an overall more `curvy' pattern of phase evolutions in Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}
L000316	(b). To quantify this structure, we consider the curvature $\kappa_n$ of the eigenphases,
L000317	  $\kappa_n(t) =d^2\phi_n\left(t\right)/dt^2$.
L000318	   In the
L000319	   limiting cases of localization and chaos,
L000320	   they follow distinct distributions~\cite{Filippone2016,Oppen94,Maksymov2019},
L000321	   \begin{equation}\label{eq:Curv}
L000322	     P_{\mathrm{POI}}(k)=\frac{\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{POI}}}{\left(1+k^2\right)}\,,\quad
L000323	     P_{\mathrm{GUE}}(k)=\frac{\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{GUE}}}{\left(1+k^2\right)^{2}}\,,
L000324	   \end{equation}
L000325	   where $\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{POI/GUE}}$ is a normalization constant and $k$  the curvature $\kappa$, rescaled by the average spacing $\Delta$ and the variance of the velocity distribution
L000326	   \cite{Oppen94,Maksymov2019},
L000327	   $k_n=\kappa_n \Delta \left( \phi_n\right) /\left(2\pi \mathrm{Var}\left(\partial_t\phi_{n}\right)\right)$.
L000328	For large $k$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:Curv} gives a power-law behavior,
L000329	 $   P\left(k\rightarrow\infty\right)\propto \left\lVert{k}\right\rVert^{-\widetilde{\beta}}$
L000330	with $\widetilde{\beta}=2(4)$ for the Poisson (GUE) case~\cite{GarrattPRB2021,Filippone2016,Maksymov2019}.
L000331	
L000332	\begin{figure}[tb]
L000333	  \centering
L000334	   \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig3.pdf}
L000335	  \caption{
L000336	    {\bf Curvature statistics} for
L000337	    the time evolution implementing the BGATE. The color of the curvature histogram  encodes the KL divergence of the eigenphase spacing. For large curvatures, solid lines indicate polynomial fits to the data, consistent with
L000338	   GUE-like behavior (shown in the inset) of a power-law decay with exponent
L000339	   $\widetilde{\beta}=4$ for all but the very first time interval which is consistent with
L000340	   Poisson-like behavior and $\widetilde{\beta}=2$.}
L000341	  \label{fig:curv}
L000342	\end{figure}
L000343	Analysis of the eigenphase curvatures in Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}
L000344	(h)--(k) corroborates the occurrence of chaotic fluctuations indicated by the statistical analysis of the eigenphase differences: Noting the double-logarithmic scale, it is clear that small curvatures are prevalent for regular dynamics in Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}
L000345	(h), (i), and, to a lesser extent (k), whereas larger
L000346	curvatures have significantly more weight in the region of nonlinear behavior,
L000347	cf. Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}(j).
L000348	To make the correspondence between the eigenphase statistics and the occurence
L000349	of nonlinear/integrable dynamics more quantitative, we calculate the curvature of the eigenphases in each of 30 equally-spaced
L000350	$t$ segments of length $\SI{0.5}{\nano\second}$. The resulting distributions of the curvatures are shown
L000351	in Fig.~\ref{fig:curv}, where the color code corresponds to the KL divergence
L000352	$D(P|P_\text{POI})$ of the eigenphase spacing, calculated for the same time
L000353	interval. Importantly, from the perspective of curvatures, there are traces of nonlinear GUE-like
L000354	dynamics for all but the initial time intervals. Despite the fact that our
L000355	spectrum exhibits only 150 levels, the curvature statistics show general
L000356	similarity to pure GUE-behavior (see inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:curv}), exhibiting a
L000357	plateau of constant probabilities for small curvatures $k\lesssim 1$ followed by
L000358	a crossover to polynomial decay of probabilities in the high curvature regime. While our data does not exhibit a perfectly flat plateau, it
L000359	does show the more defining power-law decay for large curvatures, reproducing
L000360	the GUE exponent of $\widetilde{\beta}=4$ for all but the initial time interval. The latter
L000361	instead follows a POI-like behavior with exponent $\widetilde{\beta}=2$.
L000362	
L000363	We thus find that statistical analysis of the curvatures reflects the presence of avoided crossings in a strikingly clear way. Importantly, it allows for the isolation of a subset of states for which the predictions of random matrix theory apply. Remarkably, this subset of states can comprise only a small portion of Hilbert space. In such cases, the KL divergence has difficulty to indicate the onset of chaotic fluctuations, due to the low weight of the affected states in the sum over all states. This difficulty affects also wave function statistics and out-of-time-ordered correlators which are not sufficiently sensitive as we show in the SM~\cite{SM}.
L000364	In contrast, ``sorting" the evolutions according to their curvature allows for identifying the avoided crossings that are a defining hallmark of the chaotic fluctuations.
L000365	
L000366	
L000367	\begin{figure}[tb!]
L000368	  \centering
L000369	  \includegraphics{figures/fig4.pdf}
L000370	  \caption{
L000371	    {\bf Gate error for the fast BGATE} with $T = \SI{50}{\nano\second}$ as a
L000372	  function of deviations in the Hamiltonian parameters. The horizontal lines
L000373	  indicates the gate error in the absence of any deviations. }
L000374	  \label{fig:deviations}
L000375	\end{figure}
L000376	\paragraph{Robustness}
L000377	All gate protocols investigated here exhibit remarkably low
L000378	gate errors~\cite{Goerz2017}, despite the complexity of  their time-evolution, but the transient and recurrent regimes of instability
L000379	identified above are likely to affect gate performance. For ideal protocols, the low errors are readily rationalized from the perspective of quantum optimal control: In a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, it is sufficient to know the exact Hamiltonian that governs the system's dynamics in order to derive high-fidelity control solutions. However, the assumption of precisely knowing
L000380	the Hamiltonian is not realistic. For superconducting qubits, in particular,
L000381	system parameters are typically not very accurately known and moreover tend to
L000382	drift over time. Similar uncertainties apply also to the control
L000383	fields, due to inaccuracies in the generating hardware.
L000384	Focussing  on the fast BGATE protocol where  signatures of chaotic behavior have been most apparent, we suspect small deviations in the Hamiltonian or the control parameters to cause large gate
L000385	errors. Considering constant deviations,
L000386	Fig.~\ref{fig:deviations} analyzes gate robustness with respect to deviations in the transmon and cavity frequencies $\delta_{1}, \delta_{2}, \delta_{\rmc}$, the coupling strength $g$, and the  field amplitude $\pulse(t)$. We
L000387	quantify robustness by the (generalized) gate error,  defined in terms of the local invariants functional~\cite{PhysRevA.84.042315} which measures how much the entangling content of the realized gate deviates from that of the BGATE. In other words, the generalized gate error is insensitive to single-qubit rotations because these are typically easy to correct for.
L000388	Despite this relaxed definition,
L000389	the BGATE shows high levels of sensitivity in all considered scenarios.
L000390	As soon as the deviation exceeds one per cent, the gate error is increased by two to three orders of magnitude, depending on the sign of the deviation (the asymmetry with respect to the sign is readily rationalized by the gate mechanism).
L000391	
L000392	While the lack of robustness in the fast BGATE may be expectable for a gate showing signatures of chaotic dynamics, it would be premature to conclude that
L000393	signatures of chaos in the gate dynamics categorically  hamper gate robustness.
L000394	Indeed, the gates of Ref.~\cite{Goerz2017} have not been optimized  to be
L000395	robust against parameter fluctuations, and for another two-element model system where chaotic behavior has also been observed, the situation was found to be  controllable \cite{Andreev2021}.
L000396	It will  be interesting to find out under which
L000397	conditions robust fast gates can be defined nonetheless, or whether
L000398	optimization may identify protocols avoiding the emergence of strong
L000399	fluctuations in the spectra and states of $\op{U}(t)$.
L000400	
L000401	
L000402	\paragraph{Conclusions}
L000403	We have found that fast transmon gates
L000404	display clear signatures of emergent chaos.
L000405	Analysis of the eigenphase curvatures has turned out to be the most sensitive tool to diagnose signatures of non-integrability, due to the ability to identify the subset of states evolving through avoided crossings. More conventional measures, such as the KL divergence of the time evolution eigenphases or measures based on population dynamics, are hampered by an indiscriminate average over state space. For gates that display chaotic fluctuations, time intervals where the statistics have a much larger match with the chaotic limit are followed by time intervals where integrable statistics are recovered. This suggests that the optimized pulses are able to steer the dynamics during a chaotic sea without compromising the gate fidelity. However, when allowing for variations in the parameters characterizing the Hamiltonian, the gate error increases by orders of magnitude already for small deviations. We have thus shown that dynamical instabilities arising from the transmon nonlinearity adversely affect gate implementations already for two qubits and a coupler.
L000406	
L000407	One may wonder whether there is a simple connection between
L000408	robustness and signatures of chaos. The answer to this question is less obvious
L000409	than one might conjecture at first glance. The quantum speed limit, for
L000410	instance, does not impede implementation of energy-efficient quantum
L000411	gates~\cite{Aifer2022}. Possibly, energy-efficient quantum gates at the speed
L000412	limit would be less prone to chaotic fluctuations than the gates analyzed here. Such gates can be derived by repeating the gate optimization of
L000413	Ref.~\cite{Goerz2017}, including energy efficiency as an additional time-dependent constraint~\cite{PalaoPRA13} or explicitly enforcing robustness, e.g. with ensemble optimization~\cite{PhysRevA.90.032329} or by accounting for the parameter fluctuations in the optimization functional~\cite{PhysRevA.104.053118} or equations of motion~\cite{PhysRevA.107.032609}. Generally, robustness of quantum control is key to implementing quantum protocols in real physical systems~\cite{Koch2022,weidner2023robust}. Transmon architectures represent
L000414	a particularly challenging use case, and future work will have to show whether the adverse effects of the transmon nonlinearity can be tamed to harness its benefits for quantum advantage.
