L000533	\section{Kullback-Leibler divergence of the time evolution eigenphases}\label{sec:KL}
L000534	
L000535	The instantaneous eigenvalues of the time evolution $\op{U}(t)$ corresponding to the gate implementation are denoted by
L000536	$\lambda_{1}(t), \dots,
L000537	\lambda_{N}(t) \in \mathbb{C}$,
L000538	which have a unique
L000539	representation, $\lambda_{n}(t) = e^{\im \phi_{n}(t)}$, $n=1,\dots,N$, in terms
L000540	of eigenphases, $\phi_{1}(t), \dots, \phi_{N}(t) \in [-\pi, \pi)$. Assuming without loss of generality  $\phi_{1}(t) \leq \phi_{2}(t) \leq \dots \leq
L000541	\phi_{N}(t)$ for every time $t$~\footnote{Note that this sorting might break
L000542	continuity if a single eigenphase $\phi_{n}(t)$ is viewed as a function of time}, we define nearest neighbor differences as $\Delta \phi_{n}(t) \equiv
L000543	\phi_{n+1}(t) - \phi_{n}(t)$, $n=1,\dots,N-1$. The corresponding relative
L000544	differences are then given as~\cite{PhysRevB.75.155111}
L000545	\begin{align} \label{eq:Rn}
L000546	  r_{n}(t)
L000547	  =
L000548	  \frac{\Delta \phi_{n}(t)}{\Delta \phi_{n+1}(t)}\,,
L000549	\quad
L000550	  R_{n}(t)
L000551	  =
L000552	  \mathrm{min}\left\{r_{n}(t), \frac{1}{r_{n}(t)}\right\}
L000553	\end{align}
L000554	with $n=1,\dots,N-2$ and $R_{n}(t)\in [0,1]$ which is convenient for
L000555	statistical analysis.
L000556	In the limiting cases of integrable, resp. chaotic dynamics, the
L000557	$\{R_{n}(t)\}$ are expected to obey Poissonian,
L000558	respectively Wigner-Dyson statistics. Up to inessential corrections \footnote{Strictly speaking, the distribution of maximally chaotic unitary operators is governed by the circular ensembles~\cite{WeidenmullerRMT}. However, where the statistics of nearby levels is concerned, this point does not matter.}, the corresponding distributions are given
L000559	by~\cite{PRL.110.084101}
L000560	\begin{subequations} \label{eq:Pideal}
L000561	\begin{eqnarray}
L000562	  P_{\mathrm{POI}}(R) &=& \frac{2}{(1 + R)^{2}}\,, \\
L000563	  P_{\mathrm{GUE}}(R) &=& \frac{162 \sqrt{3}}{4 \pi} \frac{(R + R^{2})^{2}}{(1 +
L000564	                          R + R^{2})^{4}}\,.
L000565	\end{eqnarray}
L000566	\end{subequations}
L000567	Deviations from the
L000568	limiting cases $P_{\mathrm{POI}}$ or $P_{\mathrm{GUE}}$  can be quantified using
L000569	the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, in analogy to the spectral analysis of the
L000570	static qubit arrays~\cite{Berke2022}.
L000571	More precisely, when comparing to Poisson (POI) statistics, a non-zero KL divergence signals the emergence of statistical correlations. Conversely, chaotic fluctuations are indicated by the KL divergence with respect to $P_\textrm{GUE}$   approaching zero.
L000572	For two statistical distributions $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$,
L000573	their KL divergence is given by~\cite{Kullback1951}
L000574	\begin{equation} \label{eq:KL}
L000575	  D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{1}|P_{2})
L000576	  =
L000577	  \sum_{R \in \mathcal{R}} P_{1}(R) \log\left(\frac{P_{1}(R)}{P_{2}(R)}\right)\,,
L000578	\end{equation}
L000579	where $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ are normalized such that $\sum_{R \in \mathcal{R}}
L000580	P_{1}(R) = \sum_{R \in \mathcal{R}} P_{2}(R) = 1$. Since in general $D_{\mathrm{KL}}
L000581	(P_{1}|P_{2}) \neq D_{\mathrm{KL}} (P_{2}|P_{1})$, we normalize the
L000582	KL divergences individually such that $D_{\mathrm{KL}}
L000583	(P_{\mathrm{GUE}} | P_{\mathrm{POI}}) = 1$ and $D_{\mathrm{KL}}
L000584	(P_{\mathrm{POI}} | P_{\mathrm{GUE}}) = 1$
L000585	which ensures $0 \leq
L000586	D_{\mathrm{KL}}
L000587	(P_{\mathrm{rel.}} | P_{\mathrm{POI}}),
L000588	D_{\mathrm{KL}}
L000589	(P_{\mathrm{rel.}} | P_{\mathrm{GUE}}) \leq 1$.
L000590	
L000591	To gather enough statistical data, we divide the entire time span into 250 intervals and collect all $R_n$ values from a fixed interval, yielding a single, joint distribution $P_\text{rel.}^\text{joint}$ for each interval. For these joint distributions, the Kullback-Leibler divergences are calculated and displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(a--f) of the main text at the respective interval midpoint.
L000592	
