L000594	\section{Further observables}\label{sec:further_obs}
L000595	
L000596	For completeness we present here our findings using analysis of wavefunction statistics and out-of-time-ordered correlators.
L000597	
L000598	
L000599	\subsection{Occupation dynamics}
L000600	\label{subsec:pop}
L000601	
L000602	In order to analyse the dynamics of the states rather than the time-dependent spectra, we introduce and discuss several measures that are based on the population dynamics and show which of those are sensitive regarding the observed non-integrability of the dynamics.
L000603	
L000604	As a first step, we consider the time occupation of the time-evolved
L000605	computational basis states $\ket{00}, \ket{01}, \ket{10}, \ket{11}$. The
L000606	question is whether traces of the non-integrability we observed in the
L000607	time-dependent spectra are visible in the occupation of computational states.
L000608	Figure~\ref{fig:pop} (a) shows that this is not the case. Tracing the occupation of a state initialized as $\ket{00}$ over the first $\SI{5}{\nano\second}$ --- an interval which we saw contains regions of regular and non-integrability in the window between $\SI{3}{\nano\second}$ to $\SI{4}{\nano\second}$ ---   we observe no signatures of irregularity in the occupation of states.
L000609	
L000610	\begin{figure}[b]
L000611	  \centering
L000612	  \includegraphics{figures/fig5.pdf}
L000613	  \caption{
L000614	    {\bf Occupation dynamics.}
L000615	    (a) Occupation dynamics for $\ket{\Psi(t)} = \op{U}(t) \ket{00}$ during the first $\SI{5}{\nano\second}$ of the BGATE. The three panels show the instantaneous, bare level occupations for $\op{\rho}_{1/2/\rmc}(t) = \tr_{2,\rmc/1,\rmc/1,2}\{\ket{\Psi(t)}\bra{\Psi(t)}\}$ of the two transmons (with five levels per transmon) and the cavity (with six levels), respectively.
L000616	    (b) The conditional population dynamics, cf. Eq.~\eqref{eq:Pown_eff}, for the same state.
L000617	  }
L000618	  \label{fig:pop}
L000619	\end{figure}
L000620	
L000621	The occupation of single particle states close to the ground state thus does not
L000622	respond to transient instabilities in the system.
L000623	We may therefore rule out the plain occupation dynamics as a good indicator of non-integrability.
L000624	However, one may speculate
L000625	that the occupation of higher lying states may be a more sensitive
L000626	indicator. A first question to be asked is how much of the total spectral weight
L000627	carried by an evolving state actually lies outside the logical subspace. To
L000628	answer it, we define
L000629	\begin{eqnarray*}
L000630	  p_{\mathrm{sub}}(t) &=&
L000631	  \frac{1}{4}
L000632	  \sum_{\{\ket{\Psi_{\rmin}}\}} \Braket{
L000633	    \Psi_{\rmin} | \op{U}^{\dagger}(t) \op{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sub}} \op{U}(t) |
L000634	    \Psi_{\rmin} }\,, \nonumber \\
L000635	  \op{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sub}} &=& \sum_{\{\ket{\Psi_{\rmin}}\}} \Ket{\Psi_{\rmin}}
L000636	  \Bra{\Psi_{\rmin}}\,,
L000637	\end{eqnarray*}
L000638	where $\ket{\Psi_{\rmin}} \in \{\ket{00}, \ket{01}, \ket{10}, \ket{11}\}$. Here,
L000639	$\op{\Pi}_\textrm{sub}$ is the projector onto the logical subspace and
L000640	$p_\textrm{sub}$ the time dependent probability that a state initialized as a
L000641	computational state stays inside this space.
L000642	
L000643	The fast gates analyzed in Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(a-c) of the main text are characterized for a comparatively large amount of population outside of the logical subspace at intermediate times~\cite{Goerz2017}. For the BGATE, for example, this  figure hovers around 10-20$\%$ at most times, without noticeable changes during the irregular time window. We conclude that the cumulative weight sitting in the non-computational state likewise is blind to dynamical instability. We thus zoom in to the next level of resolution and monitor the occupation of individual ``many-body'' eigenstates $\Phi_n(t)$ of the full evolution operator $\op{U}(t)$.  With an initial state as before, $\ket{\Psi_{\rmin}} \in \{\ket{00}, \ket{01}, \ket{10}, \ket{11}\}$, we define the probabilities
L000644	\begin{align} \label{eq:p_inst}
L000645	  p_{n}(t)
L000646	  =
L000647	  \left|\Braket{\Phi_{n}(t) | \op{U}(t) | \Psi_{\rmin}}\right|^{2},
L000648	\end{align}
L000649	normalized as $\sum_n p_n=1$. The color coding in Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}(a) and
L000650	(b) of the main text shows the distribution of these weights over the eigenphases $\phi_n$ of the
L000651	first 150 states of the system. In this representation, blue color is
L000652	used to indicate the bare eigenphases, while a combined color coding from dark
L000653	purple to light orange and dot size coding from larger to smaller dots indicates
L000654	larger to smaller state occupation.
L000655	This visual data indeed shows a massive fragmentation of the evolving state over a high
L000656	dimensional subspace during intervals where the spectral analysis flags
L000657	non-integrability (via the KL divergence). Outside these regions, the spectral
L000658	weight remains concentrated on a few states in and outside the computational
L000659	subspace. However,  while the correlation of state fragmentation and
L000660	non-integrability is a generic phenomenon, we have also observed milder forms of
L000661	transient state spreading in gate protocols without chaotic instabilities, such
L000662	as the slower operations shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL}(d)--(f) of the main text.
L000663	
L000664	While the above data may serve as a visual indicator for dynamical signatures of non-integrability, it contains too much information to be quantitatively useful. The simplest way to condense it is to track the time dependent fraction $M /N$ of the number of levels,  $M$, whose  occupation probability exceeds a given threshold $p$:
L000665	\begin{align}
L000666	  \label{eq:Pcount}
L000667	  P_{p}(t)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^N \Theta(p_n(t)-p),
L000668	\end{align}
L000669	where $\Theta$ is the Heaviside step function. Taking $p=1\%$,  $P_p(t)$ reflects the spreading/contracting of the colored dots in Fig.~\ref{fig:BGATE}(a) of the main text. Inspecting this figure is, however, also
L000670	relatively non-expressive in that it is qualitatively similar to the total occupation of the out-of-computational subspace $1-P_\textrm{sub}$, hinting at a limited usefulness of Eq.~\eqref{eq:Pcount}.
L000671	
L000672	A more meaningful quantity is obtained by  the projection of exact
L000673	instantaneous eigenstates $\Phi_n$  onto select occupation eigenstates,
L000674	$\ket{i_{1}, i_{2}, i_{\rmc}}_{\mathrm{Fock}}$, weighted with the occupation
L000675	probabilities $p_n$ computed according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:p_inst},
L000676	\begin{align} \label{eq:Pown}
L000677	  P_{i_{1}, i_{2}, i_{\rmc}}(t)
L000678	  &=
L000679	  \sum_{n}
L000680	  p_{n}(t)
L000681	  \left|\Braket{\Phi_{n}(t) | i_{1}, i_{2}, i_{\rmc}}_{\mathrm{Fock}}\right|^{2} \,,
L000682	\end{align}
L000683	Note that Eq.~\eqref{eq:Pown}
L000684	is quartic in the states $\{\ket{\Phi_{n}(t)}\}$. (A squared amplitude is hiding in $p_n$.)
L000685	We read these fourth moments of wave functions as a sum over conditional
L000686	probabilities, where the first factor $p_n$ states the representation of state
L000687	$\Phi_n$ in the actual time evolved initial state, and the second its
L000688	probability to be found in the Fock space state $\ket{i_{1}, i_{2},
L000689	i_{\rmc}}_{\mathrm{Fock}}$.  These quantities can be further reduced to yield,
L000690	say, the representation of the first transmon's states as
L000691	\begin{align} \label{eq:Pown_eff}
L000692	  P_{i_{1}}(t)
L000693	  =
L000694	  \sum_{i_{2}=0}^{N_{2}-1}
L000695	  \sum_{i_{\rmc}=0}^{N_{\rmc}-1}
L000696	  P_{i_{1}, i_{2}, i_{\rmc}}(t) \,,
L000697	\end{align}
L000698	Fig.~\ref{fig:pop} (b) shows how this data efficiently detects the instability
L000699	in the high lying sector of Hilbert space during the interval between 3 and
L000700	4~ns. In this way, it represents a good compromise between
L000701	the bare occupation probabilities of the low lying states, which are blind to
L000702	the presence of unstable regimes (Fig.~\ref{fig:pop} (b)), and the excessive
L000703	data carried by the full set of amplitudes $\{\Phi_n\}$.
L000704	
L000705	To summarize, we have found two state population-based measures, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:p_inst} and~\eqref{eq:Pown_eff}, that are sensitive to the non-integrability of the dynamics. Unfortunately, both of them require the diagonalization of $\op{U}(t)$ and their computation will therefore become difficult for larger systems. In contrast, the analyses based solely upon the occupation dynamics, i.e., both level or subspace occupations, show no correlation with the observed irregularities. This suggests that such irregularities might be easily overseen if only the occupation dynamics is analyzed.
L000706	
L000707	
L000708	\section{Out-of-time-ordered correlators (OTOCs)}
L000709	\label{app:otocs}
L000710	
L000711	\begin{figure}[tb]
L000712	  \centering
L000713	  \includegraphics{figures/fig6.pdf}
L000714	  \caption{
L000715	    {\bf OTOCs for the fast BGATE} with $T = \SI{50}{\nano\second}$. The
L000716	    operators are (a) $\op{V} = \op{b}_{1} + \op{b}_{1}^{\dagger}$ and $\op{W} =
L000717	    \op{b}_{2} + \op{b}_{2}^{\dagger}$ as well as (b) $\op{V} =
L000718	    \op{b}_{1}^{\dagger} \op{b}_{1}$ and $\op{W} = \op{b}_{2}^{\dagger}
L000719	    \op{b}_{2}$. The blue area in the background indicates the KL divergence for
L000720	    the POI statistics from Fig.~\ref{fig:setup+KL} (e) of the main text in arbitrary units. }
L000721	  \label{fig:otocs}
L000722	\end{figure}
L000723	Additionally, we check so-called out-of-time-ordered correlators (OTOCs), which
L000724	are a good measure for information scrambling as well as quantum
L000725	chaos~\cite{Swingle2018}. Let $\op{V}$ and $\op{W}$ be two Hermitian operators,
L000726	the OTOC is given by
L000727	\begin{align} \label{eq:otoc}
L000728	  F(t)
L000729	  =
L000730	  \rp\left\{
L000731	    \Braket{
L000732	      \op{W}^{\dagger}(t) \op{V}^{\dagger} \op{W}(t) \op{V}
L000733	    }_{\Psi}
L000734	  \right\},
L000735	  \quad
L000736	  \op{W}(t)
L000737	  =
L000738	  \op{U}^{\dagger}(t) \op{W} \op{U}(t),
L000739	\end{align}
L000740	which depends on the state $\ket{\Psi}$ for which it is evaluated. If $\op{V}$
L000741	and $\op{W}$ are also unitary, besides being Hermitian, and fulfill $[\op{V},
L000742	\op{W}] = 0$, we have $F(0) = 1$ for all $\ket{\Psi}$ and a deviation like $F(t)
L000743	< 1$ at later times $t$ indicates information scrambling between the two
L000744	subspaces acted upon by $\op{V}$ and $\op{W}$. While in two-level systems a
L000745	possible choice would be $\op{V}, \op{W} \in \{\op{\sigma}_{x}, \op{\sigma}_{y},
L000746	\op{\sigma}_{z}\}$ such that $\op{V}$ and $\op{W}$ are both Hermitian \emph{and}
L000747	unitary. Unfortunately, this is note possible in our case due to the larger
L000748	Hilbert space. However, since unitarity of $\op{V}$ and $\op{W}$ is not a
L000749	requirement, we choose
L000750	\begin{subequations}
L000751	\begin{equation}
L000752	  \op{V}
L000753	  =
L000754	  \op{b}_{1} + \op{b}_{1}^{\dagger},
L000755	  \qquad
L000756	  \op{W}
L000757	  =
